capabilities.json  what the collector declares in GetCapabilities, which TestTheCapabilitiesAreTheGoldenFile holds it
                   to: run the tests with -update to write it. The operator's chain test builds its stand-in for the
                   network-reach collector from this file, read from its path in the repository.

gateway-api/       the CustomResourceDefinitions of the Gateway API's kinds that the collector reads: Gateway,
                   HTTPRoute, GRPCRoute and TLSRoute. They are copied unchanged from the standard channel of the
                   Gateway API's release v1.5.1 (sigs.k8s.io/gateway-api@v1.5.1, config/crd/standard), whose
                   files say so in the annotation gateway.networking.k8s.io/bundle-version. The tests install them
                   into envtest's API server, and no test downloads anything. To move to another release, copy the
                   four files of its standard channel over these.

cilium/            stand-ins for Cilium's CustomResourceDefinitions of CiliumNetworkPolicy and
                   CiliumClusterwideNetworkPolicy, written for these tests, not copied from Cilium: they have
                   Cilium's group cilium.io, version v2, kinds, plurals and scopes, and a schema that keeps every
                   field, which isn't Cilium's schema. They stand in for Cilium's CRDs to test the watch path:
                   that the collector finds Cilium's kinds through the API discovery, watches them, and stops when
                   they are gone. The collector reads Cilium's policies unstructured, and the tests of what a policy
                   means read them from YAML, without an API server.

policy-engines/    stand-ins for the CustomResourceDefinitions of SIG Network's AdminNetworkPolicy and
                   ClusterNetworkPolicy, and of Calico's NetworkPolicy in crd.projectcalico.org and in
                   projectcalico.org, written for these tests, not copied from either project: they have the kinds'
                   groups, versions, kinds, plurals and scopes, and a schema that keeps every field. They stand in for
                   the kinds of policy that the collector doesn't read, to test that it finds out through the API
                   discovery that the cluster serves them, and names them among what it couldn't see.
